{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 979 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f93c80c7bbf0abdd44f50e86ba1015df5c658d759aa8f97a664f2f84673fe1cf" :edb/db-before-t 978 :edb/db-after-t 979 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534291 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:39:10.853Z" 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417815 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417818 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124641 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417816 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124641 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417817 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122498 13194139534291 17592186045417 false] [17592186124983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417811 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124983 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186124983 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 4}, ∂A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: x^{2} + y^{2} = 4}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $P(\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q(-1, 0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139534291 17592186045417 false] [17592186124983 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417819 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417820 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417821 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417822 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417823 13194139534291 17592186045417 true] [17592186417784